WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.160
Polymer questions usually come in four shapes.

00:00:03.440 --> 00:00:05.240
Draw a chain from a monomer.

00:00:05.520 --> 00:00:07.440
Find the monomers from a chain.

00:00:07.680 --> 00:00:10.920
Decide whether it formed by addition or condensation.

00:00:11.120 --> 00:00:13.120
And explain a property.

00:00:13.440 --> 00:00:16.239
Each one starts with the same move: find

00:00:16.320 --> 00:00:18.640
the backbone, the line of atoms that runs

00:00:18.720 --> 00:00:19.880
through the brackets.

00:00:20.160 --> 00:00:22.840
Here are four HSC-style questions.

00:00:23.040 --> 00:00:25.200
Pause and try each one, then we'll work

00:00:25.280 --> 00:00:27.120
it through, one step at a time.

00:00:28.334 --> 00:00:29.133
Question one.

00:00:29.214 --> 00:00:32.733
But one ene forms an addition polymer that's

00:00:32.814 --> 00:00:34.733
used to make hot water pipes.

00:00:35.054 --> 00:00:37.293
Draw a section of this polymer showing three

00:00:37.374 --> 00:00:40.533
repeat units, and circle one repeat unit.

00:00:40.814 --> 00:00:42.333
Pause here to answer.

00:00:52.734 --> 00:00:54.293
Start with the double bond.

00:00:54.494 --> 00:00:57.253
In addition, only its two carbons join the

00:00:57.294 --> 00:01:00.173
backbone, and everything else hangs off the side.

00:01:00.734 --> 00:01:03.133
In but one ene, the double bond is

00:01:03.214 --> 00:01:05.213
between carbons one and two.

00:01:05.694 --> 00:01:08.493
So carbon one becomes a C H two

00:01:08.574 --> 00:01:09.293
in the chain.

00:01:09.774 --> 00:01:12.333
Carbon two becomes a C H, with the

00:01:12.414 --> 00:01:14.733
rest of the molecule, an ethyl group, C

00:01:14.814 --> 00:01:17.293
H two C H three, hanging off it.

00:01:18.500 --> 00:01:21.060
Now join three of them, head to tail.

00:01:21.380 --> 00:01:23.780
C H two, then C H with its

00:01:23.860 --> 00:01:26.980
ethyl, three times over, with a continuation bond

00:01:27.020 --> 00:01:27.700
at each end.

00:01:28.340 --> 00:01:31.380
Circle any two neighbouring backbone carbons that carry

00:01:31.540 --> 00:01:32.700
one ethyl group.

00:01:32.900 --> 00:01:34.460
That's one repeat unit.

00:01:34.660 --> 00:01:35.380
Then check it.

00:01:35.860 --> 00:01:38.500
Three units means three ethyl groups and six

00:01:38.580 --> 00:01:42.100
backbone carbons, and every carbon has four bonds.

00:01:43.300 --> 00:01:46.020
The classic mistake is putting all four carbons

00:01:46.100 --> 00:01:48.580
into the chain: C H two, C H

00:01:48.660 --> 00:01:51.220
two, C H two, C H two.

00:01:51.300 --> 00:01:52.980
That's just polyethylene.

00:01:53.140 --> 00:01:55.300
Only the double bond opens, so the ethyl

00:01:55.460 --> 00:01:57.060
group can't join the backbone.

00:01:58.267 --> 00:01:59.146
Question two.

00:01:59.707 --> 00:02:01.866
A section of a polymer used in carpet

00:02:01.947 --> 00:02:02.986
fibres is shown.

00:02:03.467 --> 00:02:05.306
Identify the type of polymer.

00:02:05.787 --> 00:02:08.186
Then draw and name its monomers.

00:02:08.747 --> 00:02:10.026
Pause here to answer.

00:02:20.434 --> 00:02:22.433
First, find the backbone.

00:02:22.914 --> 00:02:25.313
It runs through oxygens and C double bond

00:02:25.474 --> 00:02:28.193
O groups, so this is not all carbon.

00:02:28.834 --> 00:02:30.753
Each oxygen next to a C double bond

00:02:30.914 --> 00:02:32.433
O is an ester link.

00:02:32.914 --> 00:02:36.353
So it's a polyester, a condensation polymer.

00:02:36.674 --> 00:02:39.073
The pattern repeats after one ring and one

00:02:39.234 --> 00:02:42.513
three carbon chain, so that's the repeat unit.

00:02:42.834 --> 00:02:45.473
A section can start partway through a unit,

00:02:45.594 --> 00:02:47.633
so don't just bracket whatever is drawn at

00:02:47.674 --> 00:02:48.433
the ends.

00:02:49.634 --> 00:02:52.273
Now add the water back, at every ester

00:02:52.434 --> 00:02:52.833
link.

00:02:53.074 --> 00:02:55.313
Cut between the C double bond O carbon

00:02:55.394 --> 00:02:56.393
and the oxygen.

00:02:56.594 --> 00:02:58.433
Put O H on the C double bond

00:02:58.594 --> 00:03:01.633
O side, and H on the oxygen side.

00:03:01.714 --> 00:03:04.753
That gives H O, three C H twos,

00:03:04.834 --> 00:03:07.873
O H: propane one three diol.

00:03:07.954 --> 00:03:09.633
And the ring with a carboxyl group on

00:03:09.714 --> 00:03:13.793
each side: benzene one four dicarboxylic acid, the

00:03:13.874 --> 00:03:15.393
same acid that's in PET.

00:03:15.634 --> 00:03:18.353
Check: each monomer has a reactive group at

00:03:18.434 --> 00:03:20.993
both ends, so each can link twice.

00:03:22.200 --> 00:03:25.240
The classic mistake is cutting just one link,

00:03:25.320 --> 00:03:27.320
and calling what's left a monomer.

00:03:27.800 --> 00:03:30.440
That molecule still has an ester link inside

00:03:30.520 --> 00:03:30.680
it.

00:03:31.160 --> 00:03:33.880
Every link was made by losing water, so

00:03:33.960 --> 00:03:35.960
every link gets its water back.

00:03:37.167 --> 00:03:38.126
Question three.

00:03:38.687 --> 00:03:41.406
The repeat units of two polymers, X and

00:03:41.487 --> 00:03:42.686
Y, are shown.

00:03:43.327 --> 00:03:45.886
Y was made directly from a hydroxy acid,

00:03:45.967 --> 00:03:47.006
with water released.

00:03:47.647 --> 00:03:50.446
Classify each one as an addition polymer or

00:03:50.500 --> 00:03:54.126
a condensation polymer, and justify each classification.

00:03:54.767 --> 00:03:57.166
Then draw the monomer of each polymer.

00:03:57.727 --> 00:03:59.006
Pause here to answer.

00:04:09.434 --> 00:04:10.833
Start with X.

00:04:11.034 --> 00:04:13.113
It has an ester group, but don't stop

00:04:13.194 --> 00:04:13.593
there.

00:04:14.074 --> 00:04:16.473
Look along the backbone, through the brackets.

00:04:16.954 --> 00:04:19.273
It's just two carbons, joined by a C

00:04:19.354 --> 00:04:20.393
C single bond.

00:04:20.874 --> 00:04:22.913
The ester group hangs off the side.

00:04:23.194 --> 00:04:25.513
So X is an addition polymer.

00:04:25.754 --> 00:04:27.913
To get its monomer, put the double bond

00:04:28.074 --> 00:04:30.633
back between those two carbons, and add no

00:04:30.714 --> 00:04:31.713
hydrogens.

00:04:31.994 --> 00:04:33.913
That's the monomer of Perspex.

00:04:35.134 --> 00:04:36.093
Now Y.

00:04:36.494 --> 00:04:38.813
Its backbone runs through an oxygen and a

00:04:38.894 --> 00:04:40.093
C double bond O.

00:04:40.494 --> 00:04:42.733
That's an ester link in the chain, so

00:04:43.134 --> 00:04:44.853
Y is a polyester.

00:04:45.134 --> 00:04:47.533
The question says water was released as its

00:04:47.614 --> 00:04:51.173
molecules joined, so that route is condensation.

00:04:51.454 --> 00:04:53.933
Add the water back: H on the oxygen,

00:04:54.014 --> 00:04:55.773
and O H on the C double bond

00:04:55.854 --> 00:04:56.093
O.

00:04:56.654 --> 00:04:58.733
That gives one molecule with an O H

00:04:58.814 --> 00:05:00.773
at one end and a carboxyl group at

00:05:00.814 --> 00:05:01.613
the other.

00:05:01.694 --> 00:05:03.213
It's lactic acid.

00:05:03.294 --> 00:05:05.373
One monomer is enough here, because it carries

00:05:05.454 --> 00:05:07.213
a reactive group at each end.

00:05:08.434 --> 00:05:11.393
The classic mistake is calling X a polyester

00:05:11.474 --> 00:05:13.713
because it contains an ester group.

00:05:13.794 --> 00:05:16.353
Where the group sits is what counts.

00:05:16.434 --> 00:05:18.753
Look for ester links in the backbone, then

00:05:18.834 --> 00:05:20.273
use the stated reaction.

00:05:21.500 --> 00:05:22.700
Question four.

00:05:23.020 --> 00:05:25.580
Nylon six six melts at about two hundred

00:05:25.620 --> 00:05:27.020
and sixty five degrees.

00:05:27.580 --> 00:05:30.540
Polyester P melts at about sixty degrees.

00:05:31.100 --> 00:05:32.620
Their repeat units are shown.

00:05:33.260 --> 00:05:35.740
Explain the difference in their melting points, with

00:05:35.980 --> 00:05:38.140
reference to the structures of the two polymers.

00:05:38.700 --> 00:05:40.140
Pause here to answer.

00:05:50.567 --> 00:05:52.446
Compare like with like.

00:05:52.727 --> 00:05:54.566
Line up the two repeat units.

00:05:54.807 --> 00:05:57.286
Each has six C H twos, then four

00:05:57.367 --> 00:05:59.686
C H twos, and two C double bond

00:05:59.767 --> 00:06:02.006
O groups, so they have the same carbon

00:06:02.087 --> 00:06:05.206
skeleton, a fair comparison of their links.

00:06:05.247 --> 00:06:06.726
There's one difference.

00:06:06.847 --> 00:06:10.006
Where nylon has N H, polyester P has

00:06:10.087 --> 00:06:11.046
just an oxygen.

00:06:12.267 --> 00:06:14.106
That N H is the key.

00:06:14.587 --> 00:06:16.346
It can form a hydrogen bond with a

00:06:16.427 --> 00:06:18.746
C double bond O on the next chain,

00:06:18.827 --> 00:06:20.226
all along the chains.

00:06:20.507 --> 00:06:23.226
Polyester P has C double bond O groups

00:06:23.387 --> 00:06:25.786
too, but no H on an oxygen or

00:06:25.867 --> 00:06:28.906
nitrogen, so its chains can't hydrogen bond to

00:06:28.987 --> 00:06:29.946
each other.

00:06:29.987 --> 00:06:32.826
They're held by weaker dipole dipole and dispersion

00:06:32.907 --> 00:06:34.026
forces.

00:06:34.107 --> 00:06:36.506
To melt, the chains have to separate enough

00:06:36.547 --> 00:06:38.506
to slide past each other.

00:06:38.587 --> 00:06:42.026
Nylon's hydrogen bonds take more energy to overcome,

00:06:42.107 --> 00:06:44.426
which is an important reason it melts higher.

00:06:45.634 --> 00:06:48.353
The classic mistake is saying the amide bonds

00:06:48.434 --> 00:06:50.753
are stronger than ester bonds, so they take

00:06:50.834 --> 00:06:52.353
more energy to break.

00:06:52.674 --> 00:06:54.913
Melting doesn't break either link.

00:06:54.994 --> 00:06:57.953
It only overcomes the forces between the chains.

00:06:59.167 --> 00:07:01.166
So here's how to start the alkene and

00:07:01.247 --> 00:07:03.526
acid polymer questions we've practised.

00:07:03.807 --> 00:07:06.926
First, find the backbone, through the brackets.

00:07:07.167 --> 00:07:09.486
If it's all carbon, as with an alkene,

00:07:09.567 --> 00:07:11.966
it's addition: open the double bond to draw

00:07:12.047 --> 00:07:13.966
it, or put it back to find the

00:07:14.047 --> 00:07:16.366
monomer, with no extra hydrogens.

00:07:16.607 --> 00:07:19.006
If it has ester or amide links, it's

00:07:19.127 --> 00:07:22.126
condensation: take the water out to draw it,

00:07:22.207 --> 00:07:24.606
or add the water back at every link.

00:07:25.087 --> 00:07:27.406
Then for a property, compare what holds the

00:07:27.487 --> 00:07:30.366
chains together, and trace that to the use.

00:07:31.567 --> 00:07:33.006
Here are the key notes.

00:07:33.247 --> 00:07:35.166
Pause the video if you need more time

00:07:35.247 --> 00:07:36.046
to copy them.
